.\" mdxfind 1.590 ENVIRONMENT section: MDXFIND_CACHE is now the only variable mdxfind reads. Drops the MDXFIND_GPU_BACKEND and MDXFIND_GPU_MAX_GLOBAL entries and the paragraph promising a family of further tracing and kernel-selection variables; all of them are gone. States plainly that backend choice, sizing, warm-up, kernel selection and tracing are decided by the program, and why: a knob that only production leaves unset is a knob nothing tests.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.8  2026/09/17 03:53:01  dlr
.\" mdxfind 1.589. Document the -n/-N digit-count desugar to mask syntax, drop the -n i IPv4 mode entirely (removed from the code; generating address ranges externally is trivial), and record that -f loads bare hex only, terminating the hash at the first non-hex character and discarding the rest of the line, while -F and -J load structured hashes with salts and userids.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.7  2026/09/12 12:27:16  dlr
.\" Document the rule-engine parity block where it changes what a run produces. Three additions. The -r entry now says nine verbs accept a character class in place of a literal character in two syntaxes, inline ?C for the John table and a ~ prefix for the hashcat one, and that ?? is the literal-? escape in both, so a rule purging a literal question mark must be written @?? and not @?, with the operational consequence stated: eight lines across the rule files shipped with mdxfind stop loading for that reason and John rejects all eight as well, so the migration costs no coverage. The class tables themselves are left to procrule(1) rather than duplicated. GPU CONTROL gains the GPU-CPU rule split, which had no home anywhere in this page: mangling rules run on the GPU, a word longer than 1024 bytes is handled on the CPU instead, the GPU walker output ceiling is 2033 bytes against MAXLINE on the CPU, and a rule generating more than that produces different candidates on the two paths. That is given as designed behaviour with its reason, that rule output cannot be bounded in principle so a limit is set and a rule reaching it does what it can and stops with no diagnostic, together with the three facts a reader needs to act on: there is no retry to the CPU, -G none is the way to get CPU-exact output, and both limits are compile-time with no environment variable that changes either. All rule verbs but S, vNC and Control-B are implemented in the GPU kernels, three and not the four the brief named, since # never reaches the bytecode at all: packrules stops at the first # in a line, so c#$1 compiles to c, verified with procrule. CAVEATS gains two bullets, one for the ?? escape breaking pre-existing rule files at load and one for the 2033-byte divergence, each pointing at the section that explains it, because both are cases where a run completes and looks clean while producing something other than what the rule file says. TH bumped to 1.583 and 2026-09-12, which is what mdxfind -V now reports. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -T lint, rendered text read rather than the warning count trusted.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.6  2026/09/09 18:54:04  dlr
.\" Document how user-defined types are listed and labelled. Two additions. The -h entry now says the listing ends with a User-defined types section printed after the built-ins, new in 1.581, and states that those rows are keyed on the id the stanza declared and shown as u<id> - the form -m accepts - never on an internal index. The reason is given rather than left implicit: the internal op of a user type is derived from the number of built-ins at start-up and shifts whenever built-ins are added, so it is not a stable handle and is deliberately not published, while the declared id is. The option letters are explained as derived from the expression slot mask: f always since every user type reads plain hex, F if the stanza declared a stored form, s if the expression uses salt, u if it uses user. Also that the section is absent with no user types loaded and that MDXFIND_CACHE must be set for userdef.txt to be read at all, which is the condition under which the whole feature silently does nothing. Second, OUTPUT FORMAT now carries the label rule, which had no home anywhere: a user type is always labelled USER_<name>, the prefix being part of the public label per Waffle 2026-09-09, so stanza [BWTDT] emits USER_BWTDT and the bare name matches only internally through the -h regex. And that a user type evaluates its expression once, has no iteration ladder, and therefore carries NO xNN suffix - the label is USER_BWTDT and never USER_BWTDTx01. That paragraph names hashpipe as emitting the identical label and mdsplit as filing both into the same ledger, because the failure it documents was the two tools disagreeing and splitting the ledger. TH date and version bumped to 2026-09-09 and 1.581. Renders with zero groff warnings; the paragraph break the OUTPUT FORMAT insert initially swallowed was restored after checking the rendered text rather than the source.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.5  2026/09/06 02:58:03  dlr
.\" Document the 1.578 changes to the -F channel. The OPTION ORDER MATTERS section said the -F before -M case fails silently; it is now refused, so the entry states that and keeps the old behaviour as history. The -F entry claimed types are chosen with -M which must appear first: measured, -m e373 -F loads and cracks, so what must come first is the SELECTION, with -M additionally restricting which selected types the file loads into. The -f entry now says the channel keeps the hex digest and discards everything after the first colon, which is how a salted hash read with -f finds nothing while exiting 0. New paragraph for the per-file load receipt, including that a file contributing nothing now says so. The -h entry documents the bare form, which prints the type table rather than selecting anything, and names the Options letters f, F, s, u and j against the options they mean. TH bumped to 1.578.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.4  2026/08/29 18:06:35  dlr
.\" Document -X, the last undocumented mdxfind option; page now covers 40 of 40. Traced XOR_key from case X at 48902 to its only point of use in checkhash at 10137: it XORs the COMPUTED digest, byte by byte with the key repeating, immediately before the hash-table lookup and before any -g rotation. The candidate, salt and stored hash are untouched. Records that a HEX[] value decoding to zero bytes silently disables the option, verified with HEX[] and HEX[zz] both reproducing the un-XORed baseline. Bumps TH to 1.544.
.\"
.\" Also records a defect found while verifying: the $HEX[ spelling is accepted and produces silently wrong results. The $ is skipped by advancing s, but get32 then writes the decoded bytes to s while the XOR loop reads from XOR_key one byte earlier, so key byte 0 is the literal $ left by the strncpy and the last decoded byte is never read. Confirmed by prediction and measurement on md5 of alpha: -X HEX[ff] gives the correct d3e8bc..., -X $HEX[ff] gives 08e8..., which is XOR with 0x24 not 0xff; the two-byte case -X $HEX[ffee] matches a predicted key of 24,ff exactly. The HEX[] spelling without the dollar sign is correct and is what the page tells the reader to use.
.\"
.\" Per Waffle, the shared letter with hashpipe -X is written as rationale rather than as a caution: mdxfind uses hx as a type-definition mechanism only, compiling userdef.txt stanzas at start-up via userdef.c 557, with no hx_compile_expr reachable from any command-line option, so no single-expression evaluator exists to want the letter. Verified hx_vm_run appears in mdxfind.c only at 13835 and 13861, inside the userdef dispatch arm. The entry states the condition so a future reader knows what has to be re-decided if mdxfind ever gains a single-expression mode.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.3  2026/08/29 17:35:32  dlr
.\" OUTPUT FORMAT: show the $HEX[] wrapper instead of only naming it. Adds two real output lines, for a password containing a colon and for one containing a high-bit byte, and states that the wrapper is what keeps the colon-delimited format parseable. Records that the form is accepted on input wherever a candidate or key is read, verified by running: $HEX[706173733a776f7264] and pass:word as wordlist candidates produce the identical digest. Cross-references rehex(1).
.\"
.\" Revision 1.2  2026/08/29 17:16:00  dlr
.\" Remove the redundant .PP immediately after .SH EXAMPLES. mandoc reports it as "skipping paragraph macro: PP after SH"; groff does not flag it at all, which is how it survived a groff-only check. macOS and the BSDs render with mandoc, so mandoc is what most readers of these pages actually see. No rendered output changes.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 16:29:54  dlr
.\" Initial revision: mdxfind(1) man page. Documents all 39 getopt options plus the pre-scanned -Y, including the 9 previously undocumented in the built-in usage text (-G -J -M -P -S -U -X -j -x). Option bodies read from the switch in main(). Highlights the -M before -F ordering requirement, the -j versus -P pepper scope distinction, the -h regex foot-gun, and -z as generate mode.
.\"
.TH MDXFIND 1 "2026-09-17" "mdxfind 1.590" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
mdxfind \- multi-algorithm hash search over candidate password lists
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdxfind
.RI [ options ]
.IR wordlist " [" wordlist " ...]"
.br
.B mdxfind
.RI [ options ]
.I mask
.br
.B mdxfind
.B \-?
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdxfind
reads a list of hashes and a list of candidate passwords, computes every
selected hash type over every candidate, and reports the matches.  It
differs from a conventional cracker in that it tests
.I many
hash types in a single pass, at
.I several
iteration depths simultaneously, which makes it the tool of choice when the
construction of a hash is unknown.
.PP
Candidate passwords come from the trailing
.I wordlist
arguments.  The literal name
.B stdin
may be used as a wordlist to read candidates from standard input.  Wordlists
may be gzip, xz, bzip2, zstd or zip compressed; they are decompressed
transparently.  Directories are skipped unless
.B \-y
is given.
.PP
If exactly one trailing argument is supplied and it does not name an existing
file,
.B mdxfind
attempts to parse it as a mask (see
.BR "MASK SYNTAX" )
and runs in brute-force mode over that keyspace.
.PP
Hashes come from standard input by default, or from one of the three
loading options
.BR \-f ,
.B \-F
and
.BR \-J .
.PP
With no trailing arguments,
.B mdxfind
prints its usage summary and exits.
.SH "OPTION ORDER MATTERS"
.B mdxfind
performs work
.I during
option parsing, not after it.  Options that load a file act on the
selection state as it stands at the moment
.BR getopt (3)
reaches them.  Three consequences:
.TP
.B \-M must precede \-F
.B \-F
parses its hash file immediately, using the type set that
.B \-M
has established.  A
.B \-M
that appears
.I after
.B \-F
has no effect on that file, which is then parsed against whatever
.B \-m
or
.B \-h
had selected so far \(em or against the MD5 default if nothing had.
.IP
Since 1.578 this is refused rather than tolerated: a
.BR \-m ,
.B \-M
or
.B \-h
that follows a
.B \-F
or
.B \-J
is fatal, and names both the file and the option that read it.  Earlier
versions ran to completion, printing a plausible
.RB \(lq "Working on hash types:" \(rq
line and finding nothing, which is indistinguishable from a genuine negative.
.TP
.B \-M must precede \-S, \-U and \-P
Those three load per-type salts, userids and peppers for the types
.B \-M
selected.  With no preceding
.BR \-M ,
each falls back to loading into the corresponding
.I global
array, which is a different and usually wrong thing.
.TP
.B "each \-M resets the load selection"
.B \-M
clears the previous load set before adding to it.  Use one
.B \-M
per group of
.BR \-S / \-U / \-P
options.
.B "\-M RESET"
clears the load set without selecting anything.
.SH "SELECTING HASH TYPES"
Until the first
.BR \-m ,
.B \-h
or
.B \-M
option, the selected set is MD5 alone.  The first such option clears that
default; subsequent ones accumulate.
.TP
.BI \-m " spec"
Select types for the
.B \-f
channel.
.I spec
is a comma-separated list of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
a bare number \(em a
.BR hashcat (1)
mode, e.g.
.B 1000
for NTLM;
.IP \(bu 3
.BI e N
\(em an internal type index, e.g.
.B e1
for MD5;
.IP \(bu 3
.BI e N \- e M
\(em an inclusive range of internal indices;
.IP \(bu 3
.BI u id
\(em a user-defined type from
.BR userdef.txt ,
matched as an exact string, never as a regex.
.RE
.IP
Selecting certain hashcat modes raises the minimum iteration count
automatically: modes 4500, 2600, 2630, 34400, 21000 and 21400 force at least
2; mode 3500 forces at least 3; mode 14400 forces at least 10.
.TP
.BI \-h " regex"
Select types whose
.I name
matches a PCRE, case-insensitively.  Comma-separated; a leading
.B !
on a token
.I deselects
the matching types.  The literal tokens
.B ALL
and
.B .
select a curated set of about 22 commonly encountered types rather than
everything.
.IP
.BR "\-h is not help" .
It is a regex, and an unanchored pattern matches far more than intended \(em
.B \-h md5
selects every type with MD5 anywhere in its name, which is dozens.  Help is
.B \-?
or no arguments at all.
.IP
Given
.I no other arguments,
.B \-h
selects nothing and instead prints the type table: internal index, an Options
column, the type name, and the
.BR hashcat (1)
mode.  The Options letters name the channel a hash of that type is fed
through \(em
.B f
plain hex with
.BR \-f ,
.B F
wrapped or structured with
.B \-F
(or
.BR \-J ),
.B s
takes a salt,
.B u
takes a userid,
.B j
takes a pepper.  A type marked
.B F
does not load from
.B \-f
at all.
.IP
Since 1.581 the listing ends with a
.B "User-defined types"
section, printed after the built-ins, covering the types loaded from
.BR userdef.txt .
Those rows are keyed on the id the stanza declared and shown as
.BI u id
\(em the form
.B \-m
accepts \(em never on an internal index.  That is deliberate: the internal op
of a user type is derived from the number of built-ins at start-up and shifts
whenever built-ins are added, so it is not a stable handle and is not
published.  The declared id is.  Option letters carry the same meaning as for a
built-in, and are derived from which slots the expression references:
.B f
always, since every user type reads plain hex;
.B F
if the stanza declared a stored
.BR form ;
.B s
if the expression uses
.BR salt ;
.B u
if it uses
.BR user .
The section is absent when no user types are loaded, and
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
must be set for
.B userdef.txt
to be read at all.
.TP
.BI \-M " spec"
Select types
.I and mark them for loading.
Accepts everything
.B \-m
accepts, plus bare regex patterns with the same
.B !
negation as
.BR \-h .
Unlike
.BR \-m ,
this also populates the load set consulted by
.BR \-F ,
.BR \-S ,
.B \-U
and
.BR \-P .
Prints the number of types selected.
.PP
Run
.B \-h
with no other option to list every supported type name.
.SH "HASH INPUT CHANNELS"
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read the hash list from
.IR file .
Suited to plain hex digests: when only hex-input types are selected this
channel takes a vectorised fast path that bypasses the format recognisers
entirely.  Types are chosen with
.BR \-m .
.IP
This channel keeps the hex digest and discards anything after the first colon.
A
.BI hash : salt
line read with
.B \-f
therefore loses its salt, and a salted type then matches nothing while the run
still exits 0.  Read salted input with
.B \-F
instead, or supply the salts separately with
.BR \-s .
The load receipt below makes the loss visible: the file reports 0 salts.
.TP
.BI \-F " file"
Read the hash list from
.IR file ,
with the structured-format recognisers and the embedded salt/userid
extractor enabled.  Use this for anything that is not bare hex \(em
.BI $ id $ salt $ hash
crypt forms, base64 digests,
.BI hash : salt
pairs,
.BI {ssha1} NN$salt$hash
and the rest.  Types are chosen with
.B \-m
or
.BR \-M ;
.B \-M
additionally restricts which of the selected types this file loads into.
Either way the selection
.I must
appear before
.BR \-F .  The filename
.B stdin
reads from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-J " file"
As
.BR \-F ,
but without a load-set restriction: every currently selected type is offered
the file.  The filename
.B stdin
reads from standard input.
.PP
.BR \-f ,
.B \-F
and
.B \-J
may be combined; each contributes to the same hash table.  If none is given,
the hash list is read from standard input.
.PP
Every hash file reports what it contributed, on standard error:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hashes.txt: 4213 hashes, 4213 salts, 0 users loaded
.fi
.RE
.PP
A file that contributed nothing says so, and says what to check.  The per-type
.RB \(lq "found N unique salts for TYPE" \(rq
lines print only what was found, so before 1.578 a file that yielded nothing
printed nothing at all, and a run that loaded no hashes looked exactly like a
run that loaded them and cracked none.
.SH "SALTS, USERIDS, PEPPERS AND KEYS"
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
Load salts into the global salt array, one per line.
.TP
.BI \-u " file"
Load userids into the global userid array, one per line.  Empty lines are
ignored.
.TP
.BI \-j " file"
Load
.I peppers
into the global pepper array, one per line.  A global pepper is tried
against every selected type that takes one; it is not associated with any
particular type.
.TP
.BI \-P " file"
Load peppers for the types named by the preceding
.BR \-M .
With no preceding
.BR \-M ,
.B \-P
degenerates to exactly what
.B \-j
does and loads the global array instead.
.IP
.B \-j
and
.B \-P
are easy to confuse.  The distinction is scope, not content:
.B \-j
is always global and never type-aware;
.B \-P
is type-aware only when
.B \-M
has run first.
.TP
.BI \-S " file"
Load salts for the types named by the preceding
.BR \-M ;
global otherwise.  The filename
.B stdin
or
.B \-
reads standard input.
.TP
.BI \-U " file"
Load userids for the types named by the preceding
.BR \-M ;
global otherwise.  The filename
.B stdin
or
.B \-
reads standard input.
.TP
.BI \-k " file"
Load keys (suffixes) from
.IR file .
Lines wrapped in
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
are decoded.  Each key is truncated to 255 bytes.
.TP
.BI \-x " file"
As
.BR \-k ,
but every line is taken as raw hexadecimal with no
.B $HEX[]
wrapper.
.SH "RULES AND CANDIDATE MUTATION"
.TP
.BI \-r " file"
Read mangling rules from
.IR file ,
one per line.  Comments begin with
.BR # .
Multiple
.B \-r
options concatenate their rule sets.  Each rule is validated at load time
against the probe word
.RB \(lq Password \(rq;
a rule that fails validation is reported with its line number and dropped.
.IP
Nine verbs accept a
.I character class
in place of a literal character, in two syntaxes: inline
.BI ? C
uses John's table, and a
.B ~
prefix selects hashcat's.
.B ??
is the literal-\c
.B ?
escape in both, so a rule that purges a literal question mark must be
written
.B @??
and not
.BR @? .
Eight lines across the rule files shipped with mdxfind stop loading for that
reason; John rejects all eight as well.  See
.BR procrule (1)
for the class tables and the full verb list.
.TP
.BI \-R " file"
As
.BR \-r ,
but forms the
.I dot product
of the new rules with the rules already loaded: every existing rule is
concatenated with every new one.  Two 100-rule files loaded with
.B \-R
yield 10,000 rules.
.TP
.B \-8
Interpret mangling rules in UTF\-32 as well as over bytes.  A candidate is
taken as UTF\-8 and decoded to codepoints, the rule is interpreted against
codepoints and grapheme clusters, and the result is encoded back.  Positions
and lengths count clusters, an operand may be a codepoint or a quoted string,
arithmetic and shift verbs are 32\-bit, and case mapping covers 23 script
blocks rather than ASCII.
.IP
.B \-8
adds an engine; it removes nothing.  Without it the byte engine runs alone and
the candidate stream is the byte engine's exactly.  With it, which engine runs
is decided per
.RI ( word ", " rule )
pair \(em see
.B "THE TWO RULE ENGINES"
below, because an all\-ASCII word is run by the byte engine either way.
.IP
The UTF\-32 switch is
.BR \-8 ,
not
.BR \-u .
.B \-u
loads a userid file, which is why
.BR procrule (1)
spells the same option
.B \-8
as well: it is the one letter that can carry this meaning in both tools.
See
.B \-L
below to pin the locale of an ambiguous case mapping.
.IP
The rule language itself is unchanged, and a rule file needs no edits.
.TP
.BI \-L " locale"
Pin the locale of an ambiguous case mapping.  Implies
.BR \-8 ,
because a locale means nothing to the byte engine and silently doing nothing
would look exactly like an honest result.
.RS
.TP 12
.BR tr ", " az
Turkish and Azeri: uppercase
.B i
to U+0130 and lowercase
.B I
to U+0131.
.BR turkish " and " azeri
are accepted as well.
.TP
.BR c ", " root
The mapping every other locale uses: plain
.B I
and
.BR i .
.BR posix " and " none
are accepted as well.
.RE
.IP
Omitting
.B \-L
tries the default mapping and counts the pairs where another answer existed;
see THE TWO RULE ENGINES.  Pinning it silences that counter, because the choice
was then made deliberately.
.IP
.B "\-L tr"
disables the GPU for the run, and says so.  The device UTF\-32 walker
implements the default mapping only, so leaving the GPU on would compute the
Turkish mapping on the CPU and the default one on the GPU, and the GPU's
batches are not recomputed on the CPU.
.B "\-L c"
is the mapping the device already implements and costs nothing.
.IP
.BR procrule (1)
spells this option the same way, with one difference that matters: its default
emits EVERY variant and so cannot miss a candidate, while these walkers advance
once per rule and can only ever try one.  In
.B mdxfind
.B \-L
therefore CHOOSES which variant is tried, rather than narrowing a set.  Where
the two engines deliberately differ is documented in
.BR rules32 (7),
with the verb and class tables in
.BR procrule (1).
.TP
.BI \-n " spec"
Append generated text to each candidate.
.I spec
is either a mask (see
.BR "MASK SYNTAX" ),
a digit count such as
.B 2
for 00\(en99, or a digit count with
.B x
or
.B X
for lowercase or uppercase hexadecimal.
A digit count is simply a shorthand for the mask it names \(em
.B \-n 2
is
.BR ?d?d ,
.B \-n 2x
is
.BR ?h?h ,
.B \-n 2X
is
.B ?H?H
\(em and both spellings generate the same candidates in the same order.
The form is checked strictly: digits, then an optional single
.B x
or
.BR X ,
and nothing else.  Trailing characters are an error rather than being
ignored.
.TP
.BI \-N " spec"
As
.BR \-n ,
but prepends.  A run may use both;
.B \-n
and
.B \-N
compose, and the combined keyspace is their product.
.TP
.B \-a
Email address munging.
.TP
.B \-b
Expand each word into Unicode, best effort.
.TP
.B \-c
Replace
.BR < ,
.B >
and
.B &
with their XML entity spellings.
.TP
.B \-d
De-duplicate candidates, best effort.
.TP
.B \-l
Also try each candidate with a trailing NUL, CR, LF and CRLF, and report
results in hex.
.TP
.BI \-w " count"
Skip the first
.I count
lines of the first wordlist.
.TP
.B \-y
Recurse into directories named as wordlists.
.SH "THE TWO RULE ENGINES"
Under
.B \-8
.B mdxfind
carries two rule engines: the
.I byte
engine, which interprets a rule over the bytes of a candidate, and the
.I "UTF\-32"
engine, which interprets it over codepoints and grapheme clusters.  The choice
between them is made per
.RI ( word ", " rule )
pair, not once per run.
.PP
.BR "An all-ASCII word runs on the byte engine even under \-8" .
The two engines agree on such a word and the byte engine is faster, so no
UTF\-32 semantics can change the result for it.  An ASCII test case therefore
proves nothing about
.B \-8
in either direction, and looks exactly like the option doing nothing.  Test
with a word that carries a non\-ASCII character.
.PP
Each word is classified as the wordlist is read,
.I after
the
.B $HEX[]
unwrap and never before.  The wrapper text is pure ASCII, so classifying the
raw line calls every wrapped non\-ASCII word ASCII and routes it to the wrong
engine: on one 53,976\-line test wordlist, 8,819 lines are ASCII raw and
non\-ASCII once decoded.
.PP
Each rule carries two capability bits, established as the rule file is read:
whether the byte compiler accepts it, and whether the UTF\-32 compiler accepts
it.  Neither implies the other.  An operand wider than one byte, or a quoted
operand, is accepted only by the UTF\-32 compiler.  A rule whose
.I own text
is not well\-formed UTF\-8 is accepted only by the byte compiler \(em 31 rules
of
.B HashMob.100k.rule
are, and under
.B \-8
they still run, on the byte engine.
.PP
The word states a preference and the compilers decide what is possible:
.IP \(bu 3
word entirely ASCII \(em byte engine;
.IP \(bu 3
word carries non\-ASCII, decodes as UTF\-8, and the UTF\-32 compiler accepts
the rule \(em UTF\-32 engine;
.IP \(bu 3
rule accepted only by the UTF\-32 compiler and the word does not decode \(em
neither engine can run the pair.  It is skipped, and counted;
.IP \(bu 3
otherwise \(em byte engine.
.PP
Only the genuinely impossible combination is refused, and the refusal is
counted rather than silent.
.PP
.B "What the run reports."
The rule load states how the rule set divides.  On
.B HashMob.100k.rule
under
.BR \-8 :
.PP
.nf
99995 total rules in use
  \-8: 99964 rule(s) run on either engine, 0 UTF\-32 only, 31 byte only
  \-8: 0 rule(s) the byte engine refuses were ADMITTED for the UTF\-32
      engine, 4 bad in both and refused
.fi
.PP
The second line reports the rules the
byte engine refuses that were
.B ADMITTED
for the UTF\-32 engine, and the rules
.RB \(lq "bad in both" \(rq
and so refused outright.  The four bad in both above are the
\fB@?\fP lines described under
.BR "RULES AND CANDIDATE MUTATION" ,
refused for an unrelated reason.
.PP
Read the
.B "byte only"
count as rules that ran, not rules that were lost.  Only
.RB \(lq "bad in both" \(rq
never runs.
.PP
Two further counters are printed at the end of a run when they are non\-zero:
.PP
.nf
1 (word,rule) pairs skipped: rule needs UTF\-32 and the word is
  not valid UTF\-8
1 (word,rule) pairs had more than one UTF\-32 locale variant; only
  the first was tried (use \-L to pin one)
.fi
.PP
Each is a real loss of coverage, and a silent one would read as \(lqthe
wordlist did not contain it\(rq.
.PP
.B "Locale variants."
The dotted and dotless I is the only case mapping in the covered ranges with
more than one right answer: Turkish and Azeri uppercase
.B i
to U+0130 and lowercase
.B I
to U+0131, every other locale gives
.B I
and
.BR i ,
and nothing in a candidate says which system stored the password.
.B mdxfind
tries the first variant only \(em its rule walkers advance once per rule \(em
and the counter above says how often that mattered.
.B \-L
pins the mapping, and the counter then stays silent because the choice was
made deliberately rather than behind the operator's back.
.PP
The residue is small and has been measured: on a UTF\-8 fixture of 16,223 MD5
targets,
.B \-8
recovers 15,950 where the byte engine alone recovers 2, and the 273\-target
remainder is exactly the pairs lost to this limitation.
.PP
.B "GPU."
The UTF\-32 rule path is GPU\-accelerated on both the OpenCL and the Metal
backends, across the GPU\-eligible hash types, and is byte\-exact against the
CPU.  The engine is chosen once at start\-up, so a run without
.B \-8
builds and executes the same device program it would build if the option did
not exist and pays nothing for its presence.
.SH "SEARCH DEPTH AND MATCHING"
.TP
.BI \-i " count"
Set the maximum iteration count.
.B mdxfind
tests
.I every
depth from 1 to
.I count
in a single pass, not just the final one, and reports which depth matched.
This is the option that finds
.BR md5(md5(md5(pass))) .
.TP
.BI \-q " count"
Set the internal iteration count for the types that have one of their own,
such as SHA1MD5x.  Distinct from
.BR \-i .
.TP
.BI \-g " n"
Rotate each computed hash by up to
.I n
characters when comparing, to catch hashes that were stored rotated.
Matches are reported with a
.BI rot NN _
prefix on the type name.
.TP
.BI \-X " key"
XOR every computed digest with
.I key
before comparing it against the hash list.  The key repeats as often as
needed to cover the digest, so a one-byte key XORs every byte alike and a
four-byte key cycles.
.IP
This recovers hashes that were stored obfuscated rather than plain: an
application writing
.I "md5(pass) XOR constant"
is cracked by handing
.B mdxfind
the same constant.  Nothing else is altered \(em not the candidate, not the
salt, not the stored hash.  Only the digest
.B mdxfind
computes is transformed, immediately before the lookup and before any
.B \-g
rotation.
.IP
.I key
may be given literally, or as
.BI HEX[ ... ]
for bytes that cannot be typed.  The key in effect is echoed to standard
error on every run.
.IP
A
.B HEX[]
value that decodes to zero bytes \(em empty, or containing no hex digits \(em
.I silently
disables the option.  The run proceeds with no XOR whatever, and the only
trace is the echoed key.
.IP
.B "Do not use the \fB$HEX[\fP\fB...\fP\fB]\fP spelling with \-X."
It is accepted and it is wrong.  The
.B $
is skipped by advancing a pointer, but the decoded bytes are then written
one byte later than the XOR reads from, so the first key byte is the literal
.B $
left over from the argument and the last decoded byte is never used.
.B "\-X '$HEX[ff]'"
XORs with
.BR 0x24 ,
not
.BR 0xff ,
and reports nothing wrong.  The
.B HEX[]
spelling without the dollar sign decodes correctly; use that.
.IP
This
.B \-X
is unrelated to
.BR hashpipe (1)
.BR \-X ,
which evaluates an
.BR hx (1)
expression.  The letter is free here because
.B mdxfind
has no single-expression hx evaluator: it uses hx as a
.I type definition
mechanism only, compiling the expressions in
.B userdef.txt
once at start-up, with no way to supply one on the command line.  Should
.B mdxfind
ever gain a single-expression mode, the letter is already spoken for and one
of the two has to move.
.TP
.B \-e
Extended match: accept truncated hashes, matching on a prefix rather than
the full digest.
.TP
.B \-v
Do not mark salts as found.  A salt that has produced one crack is normally
retired; this keeps it live.
.SH "OUTPUT AND DIAGNOSTICS"
.TP
.B \-p
Print the source filename of each found plaintext.
.TP
.B \-z
.BR "Generate mode" .
Emits the computed hashes for the candidates rather than searching for
them, and explores long hash chains.  Output lines look very much like
solves; they are not.  This is a test and bootstrap mode \(em it is how you
produce a known-good hash of a known password for a given type.  Also
implies
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B \-Z
Print a histogram of which rules produced hits.
.TP
.BI \-W " spec"
Wordlist ETA control.
.I spec
is a line count with an optional
.BR K ,
.B M
or
.B G
suffix;
.B auto
to count lines in a background thread;
.BI auto: path
to do so with an explicit cache database; or
.B none
to disable the estimate.  Setting
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
enables
.B auto
implicitly;
.B \-W none
overrides that.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the program version and exit.  The exit status is 1.
.TP
.B \-Y
Load
.B userdef.txt
(see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ),
print the load report, and exit.  A run without
.B \-Y
loads the same file silently.  This option is recognised by a pre-scan of
the argument vector, before ordinary option parsing.
.TP
.B \-?
Print the usage summary and exit.
.SH "GPU CONTROL"
.TP
.BI \-G " spec"
.I spec
is one of:
.RS
.IP "\fBlist\fP" 10
Enumerate the visible GPU devices and exit.
.IP "\fBnone\fP" 10
Disable GPU dispatch entirely; run on the CPU.
.IP "\fBforce\fP" 10
Dispatch every eligible candidate to the GPU.
.IP "\fBserial\fP" 10
Initialise devices one at a time rather than in parallel.
.IP "\fIlist\fP" 10
A comma-separated list of device indices and ranges, e.g.
.B 0,2,4
or
.BR 0\-2,5,7\-9 .
.RE
.IP
On a build with no GPU support,
.B \-G
with a device list is a fatal error.
.IP
Mangling rules run on the GPU as well.  A word longer than 1024 bytes is
handled on the CPU instead, and the GPU rule walker's output ceiling is 2033
bytes against MAXLINE on the CPU, so a rule that generates more than that
produces different candidates on the two paths.  That is designed
behaviour: rule output cannot be bounded in principle, so a limit is set and
a rule reaching it does what it can and stops, with no diagnostic.  There is
no retry to the CPU.  Use
.B "\-G none"
when CPU-exact output is required.  Both limits are compile-time; no
environment variable changes either.  All rule verbs but
.BR S ,
.B vNC
and Control-B are implemented in the GPU kernels; a rule using one of those
three routes to the CPU while the rest of the set still runs on the GPU, and
the reported hit set is the union of the two.
.TP
.BI \-t " count"
Worker thread count.  Defaults to the number of online processors.  Valid
range is 1 to 32768.
.SH "MASK SYNTAX"
Masks are accepted by
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-N ,
and as a lone trailing argument for brute-force mode.  A mask is a sequence
of literal bytes and class references.
.RS
.TP 6
.B ?d
digits 0\-9
.TP
.B ?l
lowercase a\-z
.TP
.B ?u
uppercase A\-Z
.TP
.B ?s
printable non-alphanumerics, including space
.TP
.B ?a
all printable, 0x20\-0x7e
.TP
.B ?b
all byte values, 0x00\-0xff
.TP
.B ?h
lowercase hex, 0\-9a\-f
.TP
.B ?H
uppercase hex, 0\-9A\-F
.RE
.PP
A custom class is written
.BI ?[ set ]\fR,
where
.I set
is a list of literal characters, ranges such as
.BR a\-f ,
and escapes:
.BI \e\ex NN
for a hexadecimal byte,
.BI \e NNN
for an octal byte, and
.BI \e c
for a literal
.IR c .
Any character not introduced by
.B ?
is a literal.
.PP
Masks may be up to 256 positions.  GPU dispatch handles at most 16 variable
positions per side; longer masks are computed on the CPU and a notice is
printed.
.SH "OUTPUT FORMAT"
A solve is written to standard output as
.PP
.RS
.I TYPE
.BI x NN
.I hash\fB:\fIpassword
.RE
.PP
for an unsalted type, or
.PP
.RS
.I TYPE
.BI x NN
.I hash\fB:\fIsalt\fB:\fIpassword
.RE
.PP
for a salted one.
.I NN
is the zero-padded iteration depth at which the match occurred, so
.B MD5x03
means
.BR md5(md5(md5(pass))) .
.PP
A user-defined type is always labelled
.BI USER_ name
on output \(em the
.B USER_
prefix is part of the public label, so a type whose stanza header is
.B [BWTDT]
emits
.BR USER_BWTDT .
The bare stanza name is matched only internally, by
.BR \-h 's
regex.  A user type evaluates its
.BR hx (7)
expression once and has no iteration ladder, so there is no depth to report and
.B no
.BI x NN
.B suffix is written:
the label is
.B USER_BWTDT
and never
.BR USER_BWTDTx01 .
.BR hashpipe (1)
emits the identical label, and
.BR mdsplit (1)
therefore files both tools' output into the same
.BI name .USER_ type
ledger.
.PP
A password containing bytes that do not survive a line-oriented format \(em
a colon, a control character, a high-bit byte \(em is emitted wrapped in
.BI $HEX[ ... ]\fR,
holding the password's bytes in lowercase hexadecimal.  Both of these are
real output lines, the first for the password
.BR pass:word ,
the second for
.B cafe
with an acute accent on the final e:
.PP
.RS
.nf
MD5x01 48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]
MD5x01 07117fe4a1ebd544965dc19573183da2:$HEX[636166c3a9]
.fi
.RE
.PP
The wrapper is what keeps the colon-delimited format parseable when the
password itself contains a colon.  It is accepted on input wherever a
candidate or a key is read, so results can be fed straight back in as a
wordlist.  See
.BR rehex (1)
to apply or remove it.
.PP
Progress, counts and diagnostics go to standard error, so the two streams
may be separated safely.
.PP
.BR mdsplit (1)
consumes this format.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
Path to the
.B mdxfind.db
SQLite line-count cache.  Setting it implicitly enables
.BR "\-W auto" .
Its
.I directory component
is also where
.B userdef.txt
and the compiled GPU kernel cache are looked up; if the value contains no
directory separator, the current directory is used.  With
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
unset, no user-defined types are loaded and the GPU kernel cache is
disabled with a warning.
.PP
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
is the only environment variable
.B mdxfind
reads.  Nothing else about a run is settable from the environment: the GPU
backend, work-group and page sizing, warm-up, kernel selection and every
tracing channel are decided by the program from the work in front of it.
Earlier versions carried some forty
.BR MDXFIND_ *
variables for tracing and kernel selection; they were removed in 1.590
because a knob that only production leaves unset is a knob nothing tests.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B userdef.txt
User-defined hash types, one INI-style stanza per type, each carrying an
.BR hx (1)
expression.  Located in the directory component of
.BR MDXFIND_CACHE .
Validate it with
.BR "mdxfind \-Y" .
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B mdxfind
exits 0 on normal completion, whether or not anything was found.  It exits
1 on a usage error, an unreadable input file, an unknown type selector, and
after
.BR \-V .
.SH EXAMPLES
Test a hex hash list against a wordlist for MD5, SHA1 and NTLM at three
iteration depths:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mdxfind \-m e1,e8,1000 \-i 3 \-f hashes.txt wordlist.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Crack a file of
.B $2y$
bcrypt hashes.  bcrypt carries its salt inside the hash, so the structured
channel is required, and
.B \-M
comes first:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mdxfind \-M 3200 \-F bcrypt.txt wordlist.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Try a per-type pepper against two salted MD5 constructions, and a separate
global pepper across everything:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mdxfind \-M e10,e11 \-P site\-peppers.txt \e
        \-j global\-peppers.txt \-F hashes.txt wordlist.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate the SHA1 of a known password to confirm a type is what you think
it is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo password123 | mdxfind \-m e8 \-z \-f /dev/null stdin
.fi
.RE
.PP
Brute-force an eight-character lowercase-plus-digit keyspace:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mdxfind \-m e1 \-f hashes.txt '?l?l?l?l?l?l?d?d'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Append a two-digit year to every candidate, with a rule file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mdxfind \-m e1 \-r best64.rule \-n '19?d' \-f hashes.txt words.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-h
is a regex, not help.  See
.BR "SELECTING HASH TYPES" .
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-M
after
.B \-F
fails silently.  See
.BR "OPTION ORDER MATTERS" .
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-z
generates hashes; it does not solve them.  Output that looks like a crack
is arithmetic, not a discovery.
.IP \(bu 3
The
.B r
mangling rule reverses
.IR bytes ,
not code points, and will produce mojibake on non-ASCII input.  Under
.B \-8
it reverses grapheme clusters instead, which is the fix; without
.B \-8
generate reversals externally if the wordlist is not ASCII.
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-f
pointed at an empty file, without
.BR \-z ,
is a guaranteed no-op that will still consume the entire wordlist.
.IP \(bu 3
.B ??
is now the literal-\c
.B ?
escape in a mangling rule.  A rule file written before this change that
purges or substitutes a literal question mark will have those lines refused
at load.  See
.BR "RULES AND CANDIDATE MUTATION" .
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-8
is the UTF\-32 rule switch.
.B \-u
is a userid file, which is why the UTF\-32 switch is
.B \-8
in both tools.
See
.BR "THE TWO RULE ENGINES" .
.IP \(bu 3
Under
.B \-8
an all\-ASCII word is still run by the byte engine, so an ASCII test case
cannot show the option working or failing.  See
.BR "THE TWO RULE ENGINES" .
.IP \(bu 3
The GPU and CPU rule engines truncate rule output at different lengths, so a
rule generating more than 2033 bytes yields different candidates on each, and
under
.B \-8
that limit counts codepoints rather than bytes.
.B \-G none
is the only way to guarantee CPU-exact output.  See
.BR "GPU CONTROL" .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR hashpipe (1),
.BR mdsplit (1),
.BR procrule (1),
.BR rules32 (7),
.BR rling (1),
.BR getpass (1),
.BR hx (1)
.PP
The mm-macro reference manual for
.B mdxfind
covers the algorithm catalog, the internal type numbering and the hash
